"DB-SpMSpV: Dual-View Blocked Sparse Matrix-Sparse Vector Multiplication for Dynamic GPU Workloads (https://arxiv.org/html/2608.16308v1)\nciteturn2view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2608.16308v1\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 512\nL0: ##### Report GitHub Issue\nL1: \nL2: [Button: ×]\nL3: \nL4: Title: [Input: Enter title]\nL5: \nL6: Content selection saved. Describe the issue below:\nL7: \nL8: Description:\nL9: \nL10: [Button: Submit without GitHub] [Button: Submit in GitHub]\nL11: \nL12: 【0†Why HTML?†info.arxiv.org】 Report Issue 【1†Back to Abstract】 【2†Download PDF】 Contents Reading mode Colour theme\nL13:   1. 【3†Abstract.】\nL14:   2. 【4†1 Introduction】\nL15:   3. 【5†2 Background and Motivation】\nL16:     1. 【6†2.1 SpMSpV】\nL17:     2. 【7†2.2 Motivation: Need for Two-Level Adaptivity】\nL18:   4. 【8†3 DB-SpMSpV】\nL19:     1. 【9†3.1 Overview】\nL20:     2. 【10†3.2 Dual-view sparse storage format】\nL21:       1. 【11†3.2.1 Sparse matrix storage format】\nL22:       2. 【12†3.2.2 Sparse vector storage format】\nL23:     3. 【13†3.3 DB-SpMSpV Algorithm】\nL24:       1. 【14†3.3.1 Dual-Path Adaptive Execution】\nL25:       2. 【15†3.3.2 Path-Aware Load Balancing】\nL26:       3. 【16†3.3.3 Memory-Latency Hiding and Hierarchical Writeback】\nL27:   5. 【17†4 Application-Level Integration】\nL28:     1. 【18†4.1 Efficient Construction of Vector Metadata】\nL29:     2. 【19†4.2 DB-BFS】\nL30:     3. 【20†4.3 DB-Decoding】\nL31:   6. 【21†5 Evaluation】\nL32:     1. 【22†5.1 Experimental Setup】\nL33:     2. 【23†5.2 Performance of SpMSpV】\nL34:     3. 【24†5.3 Application Performance】\nL35:     4. 【25†5.4 Representative Analysis & Ablations】\nL36:     5. 【26†5.5 Overhead】\nL37:   7. 【27†6 Related Work】\nL38:   8. 【28†7 Conclusion】\nL39:   9. 【29†Acknowledgements】\nL40:   10. 【30†References】\nL41: 【31†License: arXiv.org perpetual non-exclusive license†info.arxiv.org】\nL42: \nL43: arXiv:2608.16308v1 [cs.DC] 17 Aug 2026\nL44: \nL45: 1\nL46: # DB-SpMSpV: Dual-View Blocked Sparse Matrix-Sparse Vector Multiplication for Dynamic GPU Workloads\nL47: Conference: Proceedings of the 55th International Conference on Parallel Processing; September 28-October 01, 2026; Singapore, Singapore Proceedings of the 55th International Conference on Parallel Processing (ICPP ’26), September 28-October 01, 2026, Singapore, Singapore DOI: 【32†10.1145/3832810.3832821†doi.org】ISBN: 979-8-4007-2657-6/2026/09 CCS: Computer systems organization Single instruction, multiple data CCS: General and reference Performance CCS: Computing methodologies Parallel programming languages\nL48: Xing Cong Affiliation:  Beihang University\nL49: Beijing, China email: congxing@buaa.edu.cn , Chenhao Xie Note: Corresponding author Affiliation:  Beihang University\nL50: Beijing, China email: xiechenhao@buaa.edu.cn , Rui Wang Affiliation:  Beihang University\nL51: Beijing, China email: wangrui@buaa.edu.cn , Zhongzhi Luan Affiliation:  Beihang University\nL52: Beijing, China email: 07680@buaa.edu.cn , Yi Liu Affiliation:  Beihang University\nL53: Beijing, China email: yi.liu@buaa.edu.cn and Depei Qian Affiliation:  Beihang University\nL54: Beijing, China email: depeiq@buaa.edu.cn\nL55: © cc\nL56: ###### Abstract.\nL57: \nL58: Sparse Matrix-Sparse Vector Multiplication (SpMSpV) is a core primitive in graph traversal, sparse linear algebra, and sparse model inference. Its input vector is often dynamically sparse, so the best GPU execution path depends on both global sparsity and the local vector-block distribution. Existing GPU SpMSpV methods often bind storage layouts, push/pull traversal, and kernels together, making fine-grained adaptation difficult without extra storage or scheduling overhead.\nL59: This paper presents DB-SpMSpV, a dual-view blocked SpMSpV framework for dynamic GPU workloads. DB-SpMSpV partitions the matrix into fixed-size 2D blocks, maintains block-level CSR/CSC views at the high level, and reuses a single low-level block payload to support both row-driven pull and column-driven push.\nL60: At runtime, it selects the global traversal path based on input block sparsity, chooses block microkernels from the local matrix/vector block structure, and uses load balancing, asynchronous prefetching, and hierarchical writeback to reduce irregular memory accesses, writeback conflicts, and load imbalance. We further integrate the framework into DB-BFS and DB-Decoding.\nL61: We evaluate DB-SpMSpV on NVIDIA A100 and RTX 4090 using SuiteSparse matrices, symmetric graphs, and three open-source LLMs. Across input sparsities, DB-SpMSpV achieves average speedups of 5.48$\\times$–64.34$\\times$ over cuSPARSE and 2.36$\\times$–14.01$\\times$ over TileSpMSpV on A100, with similar gains on RTX 4090.\nL62: DB-BFS further improves end-to-end graph traversal by 2.66$\\times$ over TileBFS on A100 and 3.60$\\times$ on RTX 4090 on average, while DB-Decoding accelerates single-token linear layers by up to 4.50$\\times$.\nL63: ###### Keywords:\nL64: \nL65: Blocked Sparse Matrix, SpMSpV on GPUs, BFS\nL66: \nL67: ^{†}^{†}cc-license: by\nL68: ## 1. Introduction\nL69: Sparse matrix computation is a fundamental technique in computational science (【33†Anzt et al., 2017】), graph analytics(【34†Davis, 2019】; 【35†Kepner et al., 2015】; 【36†Sundaram et al., 2015】; 【37†Anderson et al., 2016】; 【38†Yang et al., 2022】), and machine learning systems(【39†Fan et al., 2025】; 【40†Ye et al., 2025】).\nL70: On GPUs, however, sparse workloads are difficult to accelerate because compact storage and irregular sparsity often lead to scattered memory accesses, load imbalance, and parallel writeback conflicts (【41†Greathouse and Daga, 2014a】; 【42†Liu and Vinter, 2015】; 【43†Maggioni and Berger-Wolf, 2013】). These challenges become more pronounced when the sparsity pattern changes at runtime.\nL71: Sparse Matrix-Sparse Vector Multiplication (SpMSpV) captures this dynamic setting. It is a core primitive in Breadth-First Search (BFS)(【44†Azad and Buluç, 2017】; 【45†Ziche et al., 2024】; 【46†Niu and Casas, 2025】), graph traversal(【47†Sha et al., 2019】), sparse linear algebra(【44†Azad and Buluç, 2017】), and GraphBLAS-like systems(【38†Yang et al., 2022】; 【37†Anderson et al., 2016】).\nL72: It also appears in sparse large language model inference, where sparse weight matrices(【48†Frantar and Alistarh, 2023】) and sparse activations(【49†Liu et al., 2024】; 【50†Cong et al., 2026】) turn some single-token linear transformations into SpMSpV-like operations. Thus, SpMSpV is not only a graph primitive, but also a useful abstraction for workloads that repeatedly multiply a reusable sparse matrix by a changing sparse vector.\nL73: Figure 1. Dynamic SpMSpV in BFS and sparse LLM decoding.\nL74: In real workloads, the sparse vector is not fixed. As shown in Fig.【51†1】, BFS repeatedly applies SpMSpV on a fixed adjacency matrix while its frontier expands, shrinks, and shifts across traversal levels. Sparse LLM decoding similarly reuses weight matrices while activation vectors vary across layers, tokens, and contexts.\nL75: Although these workloads have different semantics, they require the same systems support: a matrix representation that can be reused across many dynamic vectors, low-overhead vector metadata generation, and execution that adapts as the sparse vector changes.\nL76: This requirement makes SpMSpV fundamentally different from SpMV(【52†Niu et al., 2021】; 【53†Cong et al., 2025】). SpMV assumes a dense input vector and usually traverses all matrix nonzeros, whereas SpMSpV(【54†Shah, 2015】; 【55†Ji et al., 2022】; 【56†Li et al., 2020】; 【57†Li et al., 2026】) should exploit only the active input positions. As a result, the preferred SpMSpV path is input-dependent: push and pull trade off invalid scans, memory regularity, and writeback conflicts as sparsity changes.\nL77: In addition, as Fig.【58†3】 shows, a globally sparse BFS frontier contains distinct local regions, making purely global decisions too coarse for efficient computation.\nL78: Existing optimizations only partially address this tension. Storage formats such as CSR/CSC(【42†Liu and Vinter, 2015】; 【44†Azad and Buluç, 2017】), TileSpMSpV(【55†Ji et al., 2022】), and B2SR/bitmap(【46†Niu and Casas, 2025】) improve either row access, column access, or locality, but are usually aligned with one computation paradigm. Keeping both CSR and CSC supports both push and pull, but nearly doubles storage and complicates switching.\nL79: GPU kernels such as k-way merge(【59†Li et al., 2018】), fgSpMSpV(【60†Chen et al., 2022b】), VDHA(【57†Li et al., 2026】), HAM-SpMSpV(【61†Xu et al., 2024】), and Tensor Core-based designs(【46†Niu and Casas, 2025】) reduce specific bottlenecks, yet they are often tied to a particular layout, sparsity range, or application pipeline(【55†Ji et al., 2022】; 【62†Xu et al., 2026】; 【63†Elbek and Kaya, 2025】).\nL80: What is still missing is a coordinated SpMSpV design that supports both push and pull over a shared representation while adapting to global vector sparsity and local block structure.\nL81: To this end, we propose DB-SpMSpV, a GPU-based workload that coordinates dual-view blocked storage with two-level runtime adaptation for dynamic SpMSpV. DB-SpMSpV is built on the observation that 2D blocking decouples inter-block traversal order from intra-block data organization. At the high level, it maintains block-level CSR and CSC views so that row-block-driven pull and column-block-driven push can share one matrix representation.\nL82: At the low level, it stores only one copy of the block data and selects the COO, Dense, ForceCSR, ForceCSC, or patched-CSR format based on the intra-block structure. At runtime, DB-SpMSpV selects the traversal path using global nonzero-vector-block ratio, chooses block microkernels using local vector-block masks, and applies load balancing, asynchronous prefetching, warp-level reduction, and hierarchical writeback to improve GPU efficiency.\nL83: 【64†Image: Refer to caption】 Figure 2. Overview of General and Blocked SpMSpV with Push/Pull Computation Paradigms.\nL84: We further extend DB-SpMSpV to DB-BFS and DB-Decoding. In DB-BFS, dynamic frontiers act as sparse vectors over a fixed graph matrix, enabling push–pull switching without rebuilding the representation. In DB-Decoding, sparse weights are converted once at model loading, while token-dependent activation masks drive adaptive SpMSpV for single-token decoding.\nL85: Experiments on A100 and RTX 4090 show that DB-SpMSpV outperforms cuSPARSE, CB-SpMV, and TileSpMSpV across input sparsities, and that the same design improves end-to-end BFS and single-token sparse linear layers. The main contributions of this coordinated design are as follows:\nL86:   * •\nL87: \nL88: We propose a dual-view blocked matrix representation that unifies push and pull with low storage overhead.\nL89: \nL90:   * •\nL91: \nL92: We design an adaptive DB-SpMSpV framework that selects execution paths using both global block sparsity and local vector-block distribution.\nL93: \nL94:   * •\nL95: \nL96: We develop an optimized GPU kernel with path-aware load balancing, asynchronous prefetching, warp-level reduction, and hierarchical writeback.\nL97: \nL98:   * •\nL99: We extend DB-SpMSpV to DB-BFS and DB-Decoding, showing its effectiveness for graph traversal and sparse LLM decoding.\nL100: \nL101:   * •\nL102: \nL103: We perform a comprehensive evaluation on two GPUs across SpMSpV, BFS, and sparse LLM decoding workloads.\nL104: 【65†Image: Refer to caption】 Figure 3. Block-level nonzero distribution of BFS frontiers across iterations. Each frontier is partitioned into 16-element vector blocks. The heatmap shows the proportion of blocks with different numbers of nonzeros (e.g., (3, 13, 0.5193) denotes that vector blocks with 3 nonzeros account for 51.93% of all nonzero vector blocks), and the red curve shows frontier sparsity $s_{f}=\\mathrm{nnz}(f)/|f|\\in[0,1]$.\nL105: ## 2. Background and Motivation\nL106: ### 2.1. SpMSpV\nL107: Sparse Matrix-Sparse Vector Multiplication (SpMSpV) computes the product of a sparse matrix $A$ and a sparse vector $x$, i.e., $y=Ax$, as illustrated in Fig.【66†2】. Since only part of $x$ is active, the computation can be organized in two opposite directions. Push starts from active input entries and propagates their contributions to output rows, while pull starts from output rows and gathers contributions from the corresponding input entries.\nL108: These two paradigms expose different memory access patterns, task granularities, and writeback behaviors, making the structure of $x$ central to performance.\nL109: Push paradigm: Push is a column-driven approach (Alg.【67†1】 Top). For each active entry $j$ in $x$, it traverses column $j$ of $A$ and accumulates $A_{i,j}\\times x_{j}$ into $y_{i}$. By skipping inactive columns, push can greatly reduce unnecessary memory accesses when $x$ is highly sparse, and it naturally matches CSC-like layouts.\nL110: Its weakness becomes apparent when many columns are active: different columns may update the same output entries, increasing the number of atomic operations and writeback conflicts. Existing methods such as fgSpMSpV(【60†Chen et al., 2022b】), FastSpMSpV(【68†Yang et al., 2015】), and VDHA(【57†Li et al., 2026】) mitigate these conflicts through hash tables, local aggregation, or decomposition, but writeback pressure remains a major bottleneck as the input becomes denser.\nL111: Pull paradigm: Pull is a row-driven approach (Alg.【67†1】 Bottom). It traverses each output row $i$ and checks whether the corresponding input entry $x_{j}$ is active. This direction fits CSR-like layouts, provides more contiguous matrix access, and largely avoids write conflicts because output rows are handled independently. When $x$ is relatively dense, most row scans are useful and pull can provide stable throughput.\nL112: When $x$ is highly sparse, however, pull still visits many nonzeros whose input entries are zero. Methods such as CSR5(【42†Liu and Vinter, 2015】), TileSpMV(【52†Niu et al., 2021】), CB-SpMV(【53†Cong et al., 2025】), and TileSpMSpV(【55†Ji et al., 2022】) improve locality and load balance, but a fixed pull or SpMV-style execution path cannot fully exploit strong input sparsity.\nL113: Algorithm 1 Pseudocode of push- and pull-based SpMSpV\nL114: \nL115: 1: // Push-based SpMSpV (CSC format)\nL116: \nL117: 2: for $j\\in\\mathrm{nnz}(x)$ in parallel do do\nL118: \nL119: 3:   for $k=col\\_ptr[j]$ to $col\\_ptr[j+1]$ do do\nL120: \nL121: 4:    $row\\leftarrow row\\_idx[k]$ // nonzeros in column $j$\nL122: \nL123: 5:    // Writeback violation occurred, reducing performance\nL124: \nL125: 6:    $y[row]\\leftarrow y[row]+x[j]\\times csc\\_val[k]$\nL126: \nL127: 7:   end for\nL128: \nL129: 8: end for\nL130: \nL131: 9: // Pull-based SpMSpV (CSR format)\nL132: \nL133: 10: for $i=0$ to $m$ in parallel do do\nL134: \n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInternal Error ()\nciteturn2view1 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://eprints.gla.ac.uk/396825/1/396825.pdf\",\"lineno\":null}); \nURL https://eprints.gla.ac.uk/396825/1/396825.pdf is not accessible via this tool.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInternal Error ()\nciteturn2view2 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://ieee-hpec.org/wp-content/uploads/2024/09/140.pdf\",\"lineno\":null}); \nURL https://ieee-hpec.org/wp-content/uploads/2024/09/140.pdf is not accessible via this tool.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGitHub - Lagrange-Labs/deep-prove: Framework to prove inference of ML models blazingly fast · GitHub (https://github.com/Lagrange-Labs/deep-prove)\nciteturn2view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://github.com/Lagrange-Labs/deep-prove\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 298\nL0: 【0†Skip to content】\nL1: ## Navigation Menu