】  |    |\nL198: 【95†devenv.nix】  | 【95†devenv.nix】  |    |\nL199: 【96†devenv.yaml】  | 【96†devenv.yaml】  |    |\nL200: 【97†rust-toolchain.toml】  | 【97†rust-toolchain.toml】  |    |\nL201: 【98†rustfmt.toml】  | 【98†rustfmt.toml】  |    |\nL202: 【99†taplo.toml】  | 【99†taplo.toml】  |    |\nL203: 【100†typos.toml】  | 【100†typos.toml】  |    |\nL204: [Button: View all files]\nL205: ## Repository files navigation\nL206: \nL207:   *   * 【101†README】\nL208:   * 【101†License】\nL209: \nL210: More items\nL211: \nL212: # DeepProve\nL213: \nL214: Zero-knowledge proof system for neural network inference, with first-class support for end-to-end LLM proving.\nL215: \nL216: ## 👉 Looking to run DeepProve? Start with 【102†`zkml/README.md`】\nL217: \nL218: That's where the installation steps, model setup, GPU build, and the full end-to-end `bench-llm` tutorial live. The rest of this page is a high-level summary of what DeepProve is and what to expect.\nL219: ## Overview\nL220: \nL221: DeepProve is the first end-to-end zero-knowledge proof system for full LLM inference. It generates cryptographic proofs of neural network forward passes using sumchecks and logup GKR, achieving sublinear proving time in model size — orders of magnitude faster than circuit-based approaches.\nL222: \nL223: Confirmed working models: GPT-2, Gemma 3, Llama 2 — all transformer layers proven end-to-end, from token embeddings through to next-token argmax. MLP and CNN inference is also supported.\nL224: This repository is a Rust workspace. The 【103†`zkml`】 crate is the core proving library; the remaining crates provide the client stack, storage layer, and developer tooling.\nL225: ## Headline Numbers\nL226: \nL227: Single-machine inference proving on a 24-core / 504 GB CPU server:\nL228: \nL229: Model  | Sequence  | Prove time  | Verify  | Proof size  | Throughput\nL230: --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nL231: GPT-2  | 512 tokens  | 7.6 min  | 1.3 s  | 10.7 MiB  | 1.12 tokens/s (67 tokens/min)\nL232: Gemma 3  | 512 tokens  | 19 min  | 4.3 s  | 27 MiB  | 0.45 tokens/s (27 tokens/min)\nL233:   * 10–30× faster than the previous published state of the art (e.g. zkGPT reports ≈ 0.05 tokens/s on similar hardware).\nL234:   * Accuracy preserved: ≥99.6% cosine similarity to the floating-point baseline at 12-bit quantization (GPT-2).\nL235:   * Scales out: horizontal proof distribution and GPU acceleration are supported today; clusters of GPU workers are on the roadmap.\nL236: For the full methodology and a deeper benchmark sweep across sequence lengths and models, see the DeepProve paper (link to be added) and 【102†`zkml/README.md`】.\nL237: ## Repository Structure\nL238: Crate  | Description\nL239: --- | ---\nL240: 【103†`zkml`】  | Core proving library — model quantization, layer implementations (MLP, CNN, transformer), and ZK proof generation/verification\nL241: 【104†`deep-prove`】  | Client stack — `deep-prove-worker` runs a proof generation server; `deep-prove-cli` submits proving jobs locally or to a remote proving network\nL242: 【105†`tenstore`】  | Storage facade for persisting and retrieving tensor data; supports local and remote (S3-compatible) backends\nL243: 【106†`tenvis`】  | Interactive CLI tool for inspecting and debugging proof data stored in tenstore\nL244: 【107†`telemetry`】  | Shared OpenTelemetry tracing and logging setup used across all crates\nL245: 【108†`utils`】  | Shared utility helpers: CSV recording, memory tracking, statistical summaries\nL246: ## Licensing\nL247: \nL248: Licensed under the 【89†Lagrange License】.\nL249: \nL250: ## Acknowledgements\nL251: \nL252: This project builds upon the work from 【109†scroll-tech/ceno】, reusing the sumcheck and GKR implementation from that codebase.\nL253: \nL254: ## About\nL255: \nL256: Framework to prove inference of ML models blazingly fast\nL257: \nL258: 【55†lagrange.dev†lagrange.dev】\nL259: \nL260: ### Topics\nL261: \nL262: 【56†ai】【57†ml】【58†zk】【59†zk-snarks】【60†zkml】\nL263: \nL264: ### Resources\nL265: \nL266: 【61†Readme】\nL